The "Eclipse-Bloom" scene thrives in the abandoned fairgrounds of the American Rust Belt. Its practitioners, known as Umbralists, are direct descendants of a troupe of nomadic circus performers who claim the solar eclipse is a cosmic "tuning fork" that aligns human madness with the magnetic poles. They dress in moth-eaten 1950s usher uniforms heavily modified with copper clockwork and dark, organic plant matter. Their primary ritual involves "The Calibration," a three-minute period of absolute sonic intensity during the peak of totality, where they believe the music can rewrite the physical properties of the instruments themselves.

